    "rationale": "When the RISC-V Atomic Compare-and-Swap (Zacas) extension was implemented on RV64, support was introduced for the 128-bit quadword atomic compare-and-swap instruction (`amocas.q`), which uses `funct3 = 3'b100` (encoded with opcode `7'b0101111` and funct5 `5'b00101`). According to Section 2 of the RISC-V Zacas specification (and the general RISC-V unprivileged atomic memory operation rules in Chapter 8), `amocas.q` requires natural 16-byte alignment. If the target address is not a multiple of 16 bytes (i.e. `|address[3:0] != 0`), an address-misaligned exception (Cause 6: Store/AMO Address Misaligned) or an access-fault exception (Cause 7: Store/AMO Access Fault) must be raised before any memory access occurs.\n\nHowever, in Wally's memory subsystem:\n1. In `src/lsu/lsu.sv` line 261, the data MMU/TLB module (`mmu.sv`) is instantiated with `.Size(LSUFunct3M[1:0])`. Because `LSUFunct3M` is a 3-bit signal (`logic [2:0] LSUFunct3M`), passing `LSUFunct3M[1:0]` truncates the quadword size `3'b100` to `2'b00`.\n2. In `src/mmu/mmu.sv` line 35 and lines 139-144, the `Size` port is declared as `input logic [1:0] Size;`. The misalignment check `case (Size)` only accounts for 2-bit size encodings: `2'b00` (byte: `DataMisalignedM = 1'b0`), `2'b01` (halfword: `VAdr[0]`), `2'b10` (word: `VAdr[1] | VAdr[0]`), and `2'b11` (doubleword: `|VAdr[2:0]`). There is no provision for 128-bit quadword accesses (`|VAdr[3:0]`).\n3. Because `amocas.q` size is truncated to `2'b00`, `mmu.sv` erroneously evaluates `case (Size)` under `2'b00`, forcing `DataMisalignedM = 1'b0`.\n4. Consequently, when software executes `amocas.q` on any unaligned address (e.g. an address with non-zero offset 1..15, such as offset 8, 4, or 1 relative to a 16-byte aligned base), Wally completely fails to detect the misalignment. Neither `StoreAmoMisalignedFaultM` nor `MisalignedCausesAccessFaultM` is asserted, and Wally attempts the unaligned 16-byte atomic access without trapping.\n\nDifferential execution confirms this defect: Spike (configured with `--isa=rv64gc_zacas`) strictly enforces 16-byte alignment and raises an exception on unaligned `amocas.q`, cleanly vectoring to the trap handler. On Wally DUT (`sim/verilator/wkdir/rv64gc_testbench/Vtestbench`), the unaligned `amocas.q` fails to trigger any exception, bypassing the trap handler and branching to the failure termination loop, causing a deterministic watchdog abort.",
    "tester_prompt": "You are Agent 2, Tester. Implement a deterministic, minimal reproducer script and directed assembly test suite for the missing quadword misalignment fault detection on `amocas.q` in Wally's LSU/MMU.\n\n### Background & Root Cause\n- Files: `src/lsu/lsu.sv` (line 261) and `src/mmu/mmu.sv` (lines 35, 139-144).\n- In `lsu.sv:261`, `mmu.sv` is instantiated with `.Size(LSUFunct3M[1:0])`. For `amocas.q` (quadword CAS), `LSUFunct3M == 3'b100`, so `Size` is truncated to `2'b00`.\n- In `mmu.sv`, `Size` is a 2-bit input, and `case (Size)` maps `2'b00` to `DataMisalignedM = 1'b0` (treating it as an 8-bit byte access). It lacks quadword alignment checking (`|VAdr[3:0]`).\n- Consequently, executing `amocas.q` on an unaligned address (e.g., offset 8, 4, or 1 from a 16-byte boundary) fails to assert `DataMisalignedM`, `StoreAmoMisalignedFaultM`, or `MisalignedCausesAccessFaultM`. Wally executes the instruction without trapping.\n- Per RISC-V Zacas specification Section 2, `amocas.q` requires natural 16-byte alignment and must raise an address-misaligned or access-fault exception when unaligned.\n\n### Assignment\n1. Target Configuration: `rv64gc` (`XLEN=64`, `ZACAS_SUPPORTED=1`). Use the pre-built testbench binary at `$WALLY/sim/verilator/wkdir/rv64gc_testbench/Vtestbench`.\n2. Create Three Assembly Test Programs in `$TEST_DIR/tests/`:\n   - `test_zacas_q_unaligned.S` (Bug Reproducer): Allocate a 32-byte memory buffer aligned to 32 bytes (`.align 5`). Point base register `a0` to `buffer + 8` (8-byte aligned, but NOT 16-byte aligned). Configure `mtvec` to `trap_handler`. Execute `amocas.q a2, a4, (a0)`. On Spike, it traps to `trap_handler`, which writes `1` to `tohost` followed by `.option norvc; 1: j 1b` (clean pass). On Wally, it fails to trap and falls through to `fail:` (`csrw mtvec, x0; jr x0`), triggering a watchdog abort (exit code 134).\n   - `test_zacas_q_unaligned_byte.S` (Directed Edge Case): Point `a0` to `buffer + 1` (byte-unaligned). Verify that Spike traps, while Wally fails to trap and triggers watchdog abort.\n   - `test_zacas_q_aligned.S` (Positive Control): Point `a0` to `buffer + 0` (naturally 16-byte aligned). Execute `amocas.q a2, a4, (a0)` followed by 3 NOPs. Verify that BOTH Spike and Wally DUT execute without trapping, write 1 to `tohost`, and exit with code 0.\n3. Alignment Note for Trap Handlers:\n   - Ensure trap handlers are preceded by `.align 4` (or `.align 2`) so that `csrw mtvec` does not clear unaligned bits into invalid instruction boundaries.\n4. Build and Compilation Rules:\n   - Compile tests using `/home/rafay/riscv/bin/riscv64-unknown-elf-gcc` with `-march=rv64gc_zacas -mabi=lp64d -nostdlib -nostartfiles -T $WALLY/tests/riscof/spike/env/link.ld`.\n   - Place all compiled ELFs and intermediate build artifacts in `$TEST_DIR/build/`.\n   - Place all simulation and execution logs in `$TEST_DIR/logs/`.\n   - Write `README.md` in `$TEST_DIR` documenting the bug, ISA configuration, exact commands, seeds, observed and expected results.\n5. Oracle and Reproducer Script (`$TEST_DIR/reproducer.sh`):\n   - Use `/home/rafay/riscv/bin/spike --isa=rv64gc_zacas` wrapped in `timeout 5s` as the reference oracle.\n   - Run Wally DUT using `timeout 5s $WALLY/sim/verilator/wkdir/rv64gc_testbench/Vtestbench +ElfFile=<elf>`.\n   - Ensure `reproducer.sh` exits:\n     - `0`: if Wally DUT behaves correctly (i.e. traps on unaligned tests and passes aligned test, as expected after patch).\n     - `1`: ONLY when the functional bug is reproduced (DUT fails on `test_zacas_q_unaligned.elf` with exit code 134 or Watchdog Time Out while Spike passes all tests and DUT passes positive control).\n     - `2`: on build or tool failure.\n6. Multi-node Container & Hygiene Reminders:\n   - Use the supplied bash tool for all execution on the simulation worker.\n   - Do NOT modify any tracked repository files (`loop.py` strictly checks git diff).\n   - The test source files must exist in `$TEST_DIR/tests/` before completing 